  "description": "T1. Multi Stemmed Cypress Tree. 12 metres in height. Fell as tree is covered in ivy and leaning into neighbouring garden, previously identified as unsafe. Tree is situated at the back of rear garden in the greenery area.\rT2. Sycamore Tree. 16 metres in height. Reduce by 3 metres, leaving the tree 13 metres in height. Tree is very overgrown and blocking sunlight coming into neighbours garden. Tree is situated next to T1 at the back of the rear garden in the greenery area.\rT3. Apple Tree. 6 metres in height. Remove all deadwood. Tree is very overgrown and causing a lot of falling debris. Situated by T1 and T2 in the middle of the green in the rear garden.\rT4. Ash Tree. 14 metres in height. 8 metres in width. But back by 1.5 metres, leaving the tree 6.5 metres in width. Tree is touching building and needs to be made clear. Situated in the rear of the Barbara Bronson House. \rT5. Lime Tree. 14 metres in height. 10 metres in width. Remove large deadwood. Tree is very overgrown and subject to a large amount of deadwood, causing a lot of falling debris. Situated by the road sound at the front of the house. \rT6. Pear Tree. 17 metres in height. Tree is subject to severe deadwood. Remove all deadwood by reducing the crown by 2 metres, leaving the tree 15 metres in height. Tree is heavily overgorwn and carrying too much weight. Blocking a lot of light and causing a lot of falling debris. Situated on the entrance path.\rT7. Acacia Tree. 17 metres in height. 12 metres in width. Lateral Reduce by 2 metres from building, leaving the tree 10 metres in width. Tree is touching building and now intruding over the entrance path. Overgrown and blocking sunlight. Situated on the entrance path. \rT8. Acacia Tree. 12 metres in height. 6 metres in width. Remove all deadwood. Large deadwood in the public area. Tree is very overgrown. Situated in the front court yard.",
